WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.540
- [Narrator] It was a
dark time for “Star Wars”

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.230
when CG characters started popping

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.300
into every corner of the series.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.580
(Jabba the Hutt shrieking)

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:07.740
But before Jar Jar stumbled

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.380
into our collective consciousness ...

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.890
(blasters firing)
- Oh no!

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.720
- [Narrator] “Star Wars”
was all about puppets.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.270
Which brings us to this guy.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:15.550
- I'm Dave Barclay.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.230
I'm a puppeteer and puppet builder

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:19.430
and animatronic effects supervisor.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.620
- [Narrator] You might
recognize Dave better

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.850
under 200 pounds of fiberglass and latex,

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.596
because he was one of the
puppeteers inside Jabba the Hutt.

00:00:25.596 --> 00:00:27.040
(Jabba grumbling)

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.340
But to play the most notorious
gangster on Tataouine,

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.510
you gotta start somewhere.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.660
And Dave ...

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.210
Well, he got his big break
with a little green man.

00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:39.284
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.100
- That is why you fail.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.260
- [Narrator] Yoda was famously
puppeteered by Frank Oz,

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.880
the legend behind Cookie
Monster and Grover.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.920
- Show you near and far.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.490
- Yoda not far. (chuckling)

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.360
- [Narrator] But Yoda
was a complicated puppet,

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.910
and it took four to five people

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.520
working different body
parts to bring him to life.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.290
Dave's job was controlling
Yoda's eyes and ears.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.380
- There was a sequence when Yoda

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.780
is prodding Mark with a stick, saying,

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.020
- [Dave And Yoda] "Where
he was, what he was doing."

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.280
- And I'm doing a little squint

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.950
and subtle control on the eyes.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.750
Yup, right there, that's
me doing the eyes there.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.160
Towards the end of the
filming on Empire Strikes Back

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.000
Frank Oz was performing Yoda,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.930
and it became clear he had
to move on to another film

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:24.190
and he wasn't gonna be available,

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.600
so they needed someone
to take over for Yoda,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.800
and Frank nominated me.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.760
I was 19 years old.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.660
So, suddenly there I am with Yoda.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:33.990
- [Narrator] Even at 19 years old,

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.462
Dave's Yoda scenes blended
perfectly with Frank Oz.

00:01:37.462 --> 00:01:38.647
(Yoda yelling)

00:01:38.647 --> 00:01:39.480
That was Dave!

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:40.313
Did you guys see that?

00:01:40.313 --> 00:01:41.781
Let's take one more look.

00:01:41.781 --> 00:01:44.290
(Yoda yelling)

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.450
Dave did such a good
job, they called him back

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.680
and asked him to run a new
puppet called Jabba the Hutt.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.480
- Jabba the Hutt was
probably the most complicated

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.290
and sophisticated animatronic puppet

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.330
that anyone had ever tried to build,

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.920
and it took six to
seven people to operate.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.310
Inside Jabba, it was very
claustrophobic, very close.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.380
One person doing the left hand,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.950
the head movement and the tongue.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.110
Another puppeteer doing
the mechanical tail.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.000
Another puppeteer doing the
breathing on the air bladders.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.330
I did Jabba's right
hand, and I did his jaw,

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.480
and then I provided the voice on stage.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.340
My voice doesn't make it to screen,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.680
but if you turned up on set,

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.510
you'll hear this sort of voice.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:25.030
- Where's my torque drive? (grumbling)

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.550
- As a child, there
weren't really any movies

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:28.910
that had puppeteers in them.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.500
There was a little bit here and there,

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.990
but there were no solid
characters like Yoda.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.020
And being part of the Yoda team,

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.640
which really was the first
performance animatronic in movies

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.240
was the start of this huge growth

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.290
that lasted about 20 years.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.970
And as a puppeteer and as a puppet maker

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.910
and someone who loves the movies,

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.400
I was in the exactly the
right place at the right time

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.790
and have had a wonderful
career doing what I adore,

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.070
so I feel very lucky and privileged.

